# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r1 has "thcat_forms_only" and "frcat_refer_will" and "will_pure_law" and "theory_no_pure_law" and "intuition_from_sensibility" and "frcat_refer_will ∧ will_pure_law → frcat_form_pure_will" and "frcat_form_pure_will ∧ intuition_from_sensibility ∧ thcat_forms_only ∧ theory_no_pure_law → frcat_advantage"; kant-practical-3.logic.r1.2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3 have "thcat_forms_only" and "frcat_refer_will" and "will_pure_law" and "theory_no_pure_law" and "intuition_from_sensibility" and "thcat_forms_only ∧ frcat_form_pure_will ∧ intuition_from_sensibility → frcat_advantage"; they differ when only "the theoretic categories of nature are only forms of thought that designate objects indefinitely by universal concepts of every possible intuition", "the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will", "the free elective will has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law", "no concept of the theoretic use of our cognitive faculties has a pure practical a priori law as its foundation" and "the form of intuition (space and time) does not lie in reason but is drawn from sensibility" are true, which the second allows
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the theoretic categories of nature are only forms of thought that designate objects indefinitely by universal concepts of every possible intuition", "the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will", "the free elective will has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law", "no concept of the theoretic use of our cognitive faculties has a pure practical a priori law as its foundation" and "the form of intuition (space and time) does not lie in reason but is drawn from sensibility" are true and "the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, given in reason itself" and "the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories" are false. Is S1, "These categories of freedom- for so we choose to call them in contrast to those theoretic categories which are categories of physical nature- have an obvious advantage over the latter, inasmuch as the latter are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concept of every possible intuition; the former, on the contrary, refer to the determination of a free elective will (to which indeed no exactly corresponding intuition can be assigned, but which has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law, which is not the case with any concepts belonging to the theoretic use of our cognitive faculties); hence, instead of the form of intuition (space and time), which does not lie in reason itself, but has to be drawn from another source, namely, the sensibility, these being elementary practical concepts have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason and, therefore, in the thinking faculty itself.", true then?
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r1 has "precepts_will_only" and "precepts_will_only → produce_reality" and "¬theory_produce_reality" and "frcat_form_pure_will ∧ produce_reality → principles_cognitions"; kant-practical-3.logic.r1.2 has "precepts_will_only" and "produce_reality" and "¬theory_produce_reality" and "frcat_advantage ∧ precepts_will_only ∧ produce_reality → principles_cognitions"; they differ when only "the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories", "all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions of executing one's purpose" and "the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will)" are true, which the first allows; kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3 has "precepts_will_only" and "produce_reality" and "¬theory_produce_reality" and "precepts_will_only ∧ produce_reality → principles_cognitions"
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r1.2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories", "all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions of executing one's purpose" and "the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will)" are true and "the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, given in reason itself", "theoretical concepts produce the reality of that to which they refer" and "the practical a priori principles in relation to the supreme principle of freedom are at once cognitions and need not wait for intuitions to acquire significance" are false. Is S2, "From this it happens that as all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions (of practical ability) of the execution of one's purpose, the practical a priori principles in relation to the supreme principle of freedom are at once cognitions, and have not to wait for intuitions in order to acquire significance, and that for this remarkable reason, because they themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will), which is not the case with theoretical concepts.", true then?
kant-practical-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r1 and kant-practical-3.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3 has "frcat_refer_will ∧ will_pure_law → frcat_form_pure_will"; they differ when only "the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will" and "the free elective will has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law" are true, which the first allows
kant-practical-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: kant-practical-3.logic.r1 and kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if (the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will and the free elective will has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law), then the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, given in reason itself"; kant-practical-3.logic.r1.2 does not
kant-practical-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r1 and kant-practical-3.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will and the free elective will has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law), then the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, given in reason itself"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
